Algorithm





                            Return true if the list is empty or has only one node, since such lists are palindromes
                                by default.
                            Use two pointers ‘slow’ and ‘fast’ to find the middle node, where slow moves one step
                                and fast moves two steps at a time.
                            Reverse the second half of the linked list starting from the node after the middle
                                (slow->next), preparing it for comparison.
                            Set two pointers: one at the head of the list and the other at the head of the reversed
                                second half, to compare both halves.
                            Compare both halves node by node; if any mismatch occurs, return false, otherwise
                                continue till the end of either list.
                            Reverse the second half again to restore the original list structure, and return true if
                                all nodes matched successfully.





