Algorithm









  The idea is to distribute candies in two linear passes: one from left to right and one from right to left.
  In the first pass, we make sure that every child with a higher rating than the left neighbor gets more candies.
  In the second pass, we do the same but from right to left, ensuring the right neighbor condition is also satisfied.
  This guarantees that both constraints are met efficiently without unnecessary repeated updates.


  Initialize a candy array where each child gets 1 candy initially.
  Traverse the ratings from left to right:
  If the current child's rating is higher than the previous child, increase the current child's candies to one more than the previous child's candies.
  Traverse the ratings from right to left:
  If the current child's rating is higher than the next child, update the current child’s candies to the maximum of current value or one more than the next child’s candies.
  After both passes, sum up the values in the candy array to get the minimum total candies required.




