Approach.description:
Algorithm







    We want to find the time it takes for a signal to reach all nodes from a starting point K.
This is equivalent to finding the shortest time (minimum delay) from K to every other node, which is exactly what Dijkstra’s algorithm does for graphs with non-negative edge weights.
It works by greedily selecting the nearest unvisited node, then relaxing its neighbors.

        Create a graph using an adjacency list to store edges and their weights.
        Use a distance array to store the shortest time to reach each node, initialized to infinity except the source node, which is 0.
        Use a min-heap (priority queue) to always pick the node with the current shortest time.
        For each node picked from the heap, check all its neighbors. If reaching a neighbor through the current node gives a shorter time, update the neighbor’s time and push it into the heap.
        Repeat until the heap is empty. At the end, check the maximum value in the distance array.
        If any node's time is still infinity, return -1 (unreachable). Otherwise, return the maximum time.







        Code



    CppJavaPythonJavascript


    1234567891011121314151617181920212223242526272829303132333435363738394041424344454647484950515253#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    // Function to find minimum time for every node to receive signal
    int networkDelayTime(vector<vector<int>>& times, int n, int k) {
        // Create adjacency list
        vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> adj(n + 1);
        for (auto& time : times) {
            int u = time[0], v = time[1], w = time[2];
            adj[u].push_back({v, w});
        }

        // Initialize min-heap
        priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> pq;
        pq.push({0, k});

        // Initialize distance array
        vector<int> dist(n + 1, INT_MAX);
        dist[k] = 0;

        // Process nodes
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            // Get the node with the smallest time
            int time = pq.top().first;
            int node = pq.top().second;
            pq.pop();

            // Traverse all neighbors of the current node
            for (auto& [nbr, wt] : adj[node]) {
                // If shorter path is found to neighbor
                if (dist[nbr] > time + wt) {
                    // Update distance
                    dist[nbr] = time + wt;
                    // Push updated time to the priority queue
                    pq.push({dist[nbr], nbr});
                }
            }
        }

        // Get maximum time
        int ans = *max_element(dist.begin() + 1, dist.end());
        return ans == INT_MAX ? -1 : ans;
    }
};

int main() {
    Solution sol;
    vector<vector<int>> times = {{2,1,1},{2,3,1},{3,4,1}};
    int n = 4, k = 2;
    cout << sol.networkDelayTime(times, n, k) << endl;
}
