Brute Force Approach.description:
Algorithm





                            To convert one string to another, we can perform three operations: insert, delete, or
                            replace a character. So, for each character of the two strings, we explore all valid
                            operations recursively.




                                Represent the problem using two indices i and j, pointing to
                                the current character in S1 and S2 respectively.


                                If S1[i] == S2[j], no operation is needed. Move both pointers one step
                                back: f(i - 1, j - 1).


                                If S1[i] != S2[j], we explore all three operations:

                                    Insert: Insert S2[j] into S1. This
                                        keeps i unchanged and decreases j:
                                        1 + f(i, j - 1).

                                    Delete: Remove S1[i]. This decreases
                                        i and keeps j unchanged: 1 + f(i - 1, j).

                                    Replace: Replace S1[i] with S2[j].
                                        Decrease both indices: 1 + f(i - 1, j - 1).



                                Among the three operations, return the minimum value.


                                If either i or j becomes -1, return the number of
                                remaining characters in the other string (i.e., insert all or delete all).



                        Memoization:

                            Create a 2D dp array of size n × m, initialized with
                                -1.

                            If dp[i][j] != -1, return the stored value.
                            Otherwise, compute the value using recursion and store it in dp[i][j].






